From 2f30883ca203de9af373f46142284a9dbce340b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexandre Strube <a.strube@fz-juelich.de>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 13:48:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ai-proxy-multi): add system_one balancer algorithm

Add a `system_one` balancer algorithm that routes each request to the
instance a System One model ranks highest. A System One model, such as
TypeSafe's Jev, answers typed questions about an input with probability
distributions instead of text. The prompt is sent to the System One API
(POST /v1/systemone) as the `state` of one `choice` question whose
options are the instance names, each described by the new
`instances.description` field, and the instances are ranked by the
returned probabilities.

Unlike `semantic`, `system_one` skips instances that fail active health
checks or are rate limited, and on fallback_strategy errors retries down
the ranking without asking the model again. A failed System One request,
or a ranking in which no available instance clears
`system_one_opts.threshold`, fails open to `system_one_opts.fallback`.

The System One request goes through the shared ai-provider layer like
the embedding request of `semantic`: the client's headers are not
forwarded, the upstream body is never logged, and the answer is
validated before use. The prompt is cut to 16 KiB at a UTF-8 character
boundary. `system_one_opts.auth` is encrypted at rest.

The health-checker setup of pick_target is factored into
fetch_checkers() so that both pickers share it.
